%[text] ## Design Criteria for PID Control: Robustness, Disturbance Rejection, and Measurement-Noise Amplification
%[text] This live script consolidates practical design criteria for PID control with a focus on robustness and disturbance rejection. Using the Nyquist diagram, we define and compute the sensitivity peak $M\_\\text{s}$ and complementary sensitivity peak $M\_\\text{t}$, and we relate $M\_\\text{s}$ to the minimum distance from the Nyquist curve to the critical point $-1+0\\cdot \\mathrm{j}${"editStyle":"visual"}. We provide high-frequency approximations for measurement-noise amplification for PI/PID controllers, and we discuss disturbance performance via the integral of error $\\mathrm{IE}$ and the integral of absolute error $\\mathrm{IAE}$. All metrics are reproduced numerically in MATLAB with and without Simulink, with a worked example $P(s)=\\frac{1}{s^2+s+1}$ and $C(s)=1+\\frac{1}{s}+0.1\\cdot \\frac{s}{0.5s+1}$. The results illustrate the trade-offs between robustness, noise amplification, and disturbance settling.
%%
%[text] ### Introduction
%[text] Proportional-Integral-Derivative (PID) control remains the dominant feedback solution in industrial practice due to its transparency, implementation simplicity, and tuning efficiency. Robust performance in the presence of plant uncertainty and disturbances is often expressed through frequency-domain metrics derived from the loop $L(\\text{j}\\omega)=C(\\mathrm{j}\\omega)P(\\mathrm{j}\\omega)$, notably the sensitivity peak $M\_\\text{s}$ and the complementary sensitivity peak $M\_\\text{t}$. In time domain, disturbance rejection is commonly summarized by the integral of error ($\\mathrm{IE}$) and the integral of absolute error ($\\mathrm{IAE}$), which are directly relevant for process performance and actuator effort. This script synthesizes these notions into a concise workflow: 
%[text] - interpret $M\_\\text{s}$ and $M\_\\text{t}$ from the Nyquist diagram,
%[text] - estimate measurement-noise amplification,
%[text] - compute IE and IAE in Simulink, and
%[text] - reproduce all metrics numerically in MATLAB R2025a.  \
%%
%[text] ### Preliminaries and Feedback Structure
%[text] We consider the unity-feedback loop with plant $P(s)$ and controller $C(s)$. Let $r${"editStyle":"visual"} denote the reference, $d${"editStyle":"visual"} an additive disturbance entering at the plant input, and $n${"editStyle":"visual"} measurement noise added at the sensor. The loop is negative feedback with $e=r-(y+n)$, control $u = C(s) \\cdot e$, and plant output $y=P(s)\\bigl(u+d\\bigr)$. Define the open loop $L(s) = C(s) \\cdot P(s)$, sensitivity $S(s)=\\frac{1}{1+L(s)}$, and complementary sensitivity $T(s)=\\frac{L(s)}{1+L(s)}$. Throughout, we assume closed-loop stability. The PID controller is defined as $C(s)=K\_\\text{p}+K\_\\text{i}\\cdot\\frac{1}{s}+K\_\\text{d}\\cdot \\frac{s}{T\_\\text{f}\\,s+1}$.
%[text] ![](text:image:6334)
%%
%[text] ### Robustness Metrics from the Nyquist Diagram
%[text] **Definitions**
%[text] The frequency-domain robustness indices are
%[text] $M\_\\text{s} = \\sup\_{\\omega\\ge 0}\\,\\bigl|S(\\text{j}\\omega)\\bigr| = \\sup\_{\\omega\\ge 0}\\,\\frac{1}{|1+L(\\text{j}\\omega)|}$
%[text] $M\_\\text{t} = \\sup\_{\\omega\\ge 0}\\,\\bigl|T(\\text{j}\\omega)\\bigr| = \\sup\_{\\omega\\ge 0}\\,\\frac{|L(\\text{j}\\omega)|}{|1+L(\\text{j}\\omega)|}$. 
%[text] A convenient combined index is 
%[text] $M\_\\text{st} = \\max\\{M\_\\text{s},\\,M\_\\text{t}\\}$.
%[text] 
%[text] **Nyquist interpretation of** $M\_\\text{s}$ **and** $M\_\\text{t}$
%[text] Let $d\_{\\min} := \\inf\_{\\omega}\\,|1+L(j\\omega)|$, the minimum Euclidean distance from the Nyquist curve of $L(\\text{j}\\omega)$ to the critical point $-1+\\mathrm{j0}${"editStyle":"visual"}. Then 
%[text] $M\_\\text{s} = \\frac{1}{d\_{\\min}}$. 
%[text] Thus, on the Nyquist diagram, the closest approach to -1 directly yields $M\_\\text{s} $ via inversion of that distance. 
%[text] ![](text:image:800f)
%[text] For $M\_\\text{t}$, the peak of $|T(\\text{j}\\omega)|$ can be reported as circles around the critical point or the center in linear units or dB; e.g., $M\_\\text{t} = 2$ corresponds to $\\approx 6$ dB.
%[text] ![](text:image:6e9c)
%[text] **Measurement-Noise Amplification**
%[text] With measurement noise $n${"editStyle":"visual"} added at the sensor, the transfer from $n${"editStyle":"visual"} to the control signal $u${"editStyle":"visual"} is $\\frac{U(s)}{N(s)} \\;=\\; -\\frac{C(s)}{1 + L(s)}$. 
%[text] At sufficiently high frequency where $|P(j\\omega)|\\ll\\!1\\)$hence $\\(|L|\\!\\ll\\! 1$ the magnitude simplifies to $\\biggl|\\frac{U}{N}\\biggr| \\;\\approx\\; |C(j\\omega)|$. Therefore: 
%[text] - P/PI: $u\_\\max=K\_\\text{p} \\cdot M\_\\text{st} \\cdot n$.
%[text] - PID: $u\_\\max= \\frac{K\_\\text{d}} {T\_\\text{f}}  \\cdot n$.  \
%[text] This gives a useful engineering estimate of how sensor noise translates into actuator motion.
%[text] 
%[text] **Disturbance Performance: IE and IAE**
%[text] Consider a unit step disturbance $d(t)$ added at the plant input. With $r=0$ and $n=0$, the disturbance-to-error transfer is 
%[text] $G\_\\mathrm{ed}(s) \\;:=\\; \\frac{E(s)}{D(s)} \\;=\\; -\\frac{P(s)}{1 + C(s)P(s)} \\;=\\; -P(s)\\,S(s)$.
%[text] The integral of error ($\\mathrm{IE}${"editStyle":"visual"}) is 
%[text] $\\mathrm{IE} = \\int\_0^\\infty e(t)\\,\\mathrm{dt} \\;=\\; \\lim\_{s\\to 0} E(s)$. 
%[text] For a step disturbance $D(s)=1/s$ and a stabilizing controller with integral action, one obtains the classical result 
%[text] $\\mathrm{IE} \\;=\\; -\\frac{1}{K\_\\text{i}}$.
%[text] The integral of absolute error (IAE) has no closed form in general and is computed numerically: 
%[text] $\\mathrm{IAE} := \\int\_0^\\infty |e(t)|\\,\\mathrm{dt}$. 
%[text] Since oscillatory rejection increases the absolute area under $|e(t)|$, it is typical to see $\\mathrm{IAE} \> |\\mathrm{IE}|$.
%%
%[text] ### Fully Numerical Computation in MATLAB R2025a
%[text] All quantities can be computed without graphics or Simulink using dense frequency sampling and time-domain simulation. 
%[text] We use $P(s)=\\frac{1}{s^2+s+1}$ and $C(s)=1+\\frac{1}{s}+0.1\\cdot \\frac{s}{0.5s+1}$. 
%[text] 
%[text] **Nyquist readings (illustrative)**
P = tf(1, [1 1 1 ]);
Kp = 1; Ki = 1; Kd = 0.1; Tf = 0.5;
C = pid(Kp, Ki, Kd, Tf);
L = P * C;
np = nyquistplot(L); np.ShowNegativeFrequencies = "off"; grid on;  %[output:25e120a8]
%[text] Suppose the closest point on $L(\\text{j}\\omega)$ to $-1+0\\mathrm{j}${"editStyle":"visual"} is approximately $-0.645-0.3\\text{j}$. The distance is 
dmin = abs((-0.7 - 0.362 * 1i) - (-1)) %[output:3a361411]
%[text] which gives 
Ms = 1 / dmin %[output:4705220a]
%[text] If the peak of $|T|$ is read as $\\approx 5 \\, \\text{dB}$, then 
Mt = db2mag(5) %[output:21ab9ef6]
%[text] Hence 
Mst = max(Ms, Mt) %[output:8be76c7a]
%[text] The calculation can also be performed numerically without a Nyquist diagram:
S = (1 / (1 + C * P));
T = (P * C / (1 + C * P)); 
Hs = freqresp(S);
Ht = freqresp(T);
Ms = max(abs(Hs)) %[output:3585fa1e]
Mt = max(abs(Ht)) %[output:19a13a03]
Mst = max(Ms, Mt) %[output:877518fc]
%[text] **Measurement-noise amplification**
%[text] For the given PID parameters, 
Mun = Kd / Tf %[output:9258b417]
%[text] If the maximum noise amplitude is 
nmax = 2;
%[text] the induced control swing is 
umax = Mun * nmax  %[output:8613303d]
%[text] Acceptability depends on actuator limits and application. 
%[text] **Disturbance performance**
%[text] For a unit step disturbance at the plant input, the following applies: 
IE = -1 / Ki %[output:1dee4935]
%[text] This result could also be calculated using numerical simulation:
Ged = -P / (1 + C * P);
step(Ged) %[output:061beba3]
[e, t] = step(Ged);
IE = trapz(t, e) %[output:3f425d8d]
%[text] and the IAE value 
IAE = trapz(t, abs(e)) %[output:0972331b]
%[text] 

